A: It seems like the issue may actually be due to the fact that Pex can't deal with generic List<> in the case of the method you're testing. It's likely not compatible with generic types. 
Try using non-generic Collections like ArrayList and see if Pex can handle that. Also, please note that Pex does have some limitations specific to certain .NET types which need to be taken into account.
Hope that helps resolve your confusion.

A: As others have mentioned, it appears Pex isn't capable of handling generic collections, especially when trying to test functionality involving getting elements from the collection. 
You might want to rewrite your solution to use non-generic collections, for instance 'ArrayList'. This way, the generic collections are bypassed and Pex might execute better. 
Unfortunately, there don't appear to be built-in ways to force Pex to use non-generic collections, unless you want to modify the Pex source code.
Hope this at least provides some further insight into the issue and helps you to find a solution.

A: If Pex has limitations in handling generic types and ArrayList is not an option, one way to achieve higher code coverage in your test would be to create a set of test cases that exercise only non-generic members of your API.
If your API has many non-generic methods you could test these first before moving onto the generic ones.
For covering ArrayList you could create tests for methods like Add, Remove and Clear etc., then for generic methods you might only test for Add, Remove and Clear methods if your generic methods delegate to these non-generic methods.
Testing non-generic methods first will allow you to reach more lines of code in your tests for ArrayList than generic methods, possibly leading to higher code coverage. 
Remember, coverage metrics should enable you to identify the areas of your code that are not yet being covered and focus your efforts there. 
Note: You should keep in mind that you'll be writing less 'interesting' test cases, but they will at least ensure that the core functionality isn't broken.
This solution is essentially the way industry recommends when testing Microsoft's code as it should pass as long as the non-generic code works correctly.

