A: Based on your requirement, it seems like you need an automated testing tool like NUnit or MSTest which could easily be integrated with TestDriven.Net. This would allow you to write small, self-contained tests and run them automatically with every build.
The benefit to automated testing is that you can run these tests quite frequently (automated builds) and have a high level of confidence that your application and its API will work if you make small changes to it.
An example test could look something like this:
[Test]
public void List_ContainsElement_ReturnsTrueWhenContainsItem()
{
    //Arrange 
    var testList = new List<string>() { "test" };

    //Act
    var result = testList.Contains("test");

    //Assert
    Assert.IsTrue(result);
}

For ArrayList you would replace List<T> with ArrayList and string with your element type. You should use the Assert class to check if a particular condition is met (e.g. using Assert.IsTrue for the 'IsTrue' condition in this case).
Lastly, test coverage tools like dotCover or NCover would give you statistics on what parts of your code are currently covered by your tests. This can give you a high-level feeling of how well your tests cover your application.
I hope this helps, if you have further questions simply let me know.

A: Your question doesn't provide enough information about your requirements or what kind of testing you're looking into. 

If you are looking for unit tests for ArrayList methods like add, remove, insert etc., then you can use a mocking / stubbing framework like Moq or Rhino Mocks to create fake data for your unit tests. Nunit or xUnit are good frameworks to use for unit testing.

For example, here is a simple NUnit unit test:
[Test]
public void TestArrayListAdd()
{
    ArrayList array = new ArrayList();
    array.Add("Test");
    Assert.AreEqual(1, array.Count);
}

In the above test case, ArrayList object is created and method "Add" is called to add an item, and then we simply check whether the number of items in the list is 1 using the "Count" property. If the number of items in the array list is 1, then the test case passes.

If you want to test how your ArrayList handles an exception, you can use a try-catch block and assert the exception type:
[Test]
public void TestArrayListException()
{
    ArrayList array = new ArrayList();
    try
    {
        array[2] = "Test";
    }
    catch (IndexOutOfRangeException ex)
    {
        Assert.AreEqual("Index was outside the bounds of the array.", ex.Message);
    }
}

In case of exception,--------------------	    Debug.Assert(Old_index <= Old_arrListCount, "@@@ old index is less than or same as old array count");

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ "index out of range" and new array list count is still same as old array list count.
	    Debug.Assert(index < Old_arrListCount && index >= 0, "@@@ index out of range and new array list count is still same as old array list count");

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that "Element retrieved is same as put element"
	    Debug.Assert(New_Ret == arrList[Old_index], "Element retrieved is same as put element");
        }
    }
}
```

[Test method PUT_GetContract]

This test method tests the `GET` property contract of the `ArrayList` class. The contract of this property is: 

```
GET : Returns the element at the specified index.
```
 
The test is parameterized with an `ArrayList` and an integer `index`. The assumption of the test is that array index is within the bounds of the ArrayList, as `ArrayList` is not allowed to access elements out of range. The observed values are `Old_arrListCount` – the count of elements in the `ArrayList` before the access, `Old_index` – index of the retrieved element before the access, `New_Ret` – the returned value by the `GET` access, `New_arrListCount` – the count of elements in the `ArrayList` after the access and `New_containsNewRet` – whether the new `ArrayList` contains the retrieved element.

The test uses two assertions:

1. The first assertion uses "Debug.Assert(.*?);" that ensures index is within the range of the array. 
2. The second assertion uses "Debug.Assert(.*?);" that ensures the element retrieved from the array is the one we put in.

If a test fails, the associated observable values, error messages, and stack trace information will be displayed in the test results in Visual Studio. This can help developers to diagnose what went wrong in the test.

[Test method PUT_SetContract]

This test method tests the `SET` property contract of the `ArrayList` class. The contract of this property is:

```
SET : Sets the element at the specified index.
```

The test is parameterized with an `ArrayList`, an index, and an object to insert at the index. The assumption of the test is that array index is within the bounds of the ArrayList, as `ArrayList` is not allowed to insert/update out-of-range indicies.

The observed values are `Old_arrListCount` – the count of elements in the `ArrayList` before the SET operation, `Old_index` – index at which the value is replaced, `Old_value` – the value that was being replaced before the operation, and `New_value` – the new value that was set.

The test uses two assertions:

1. The first assertion "Debug.Assert(.*?);" that ensures index is within the range of the array.
2. The second assertion "Debug.Assert(.*?);" that ensures the element that was replaced by the SET operation is the old one and the new element is the one which was set.

In case of failure, the developer can see the associated observable values, error messages, and stack trace information in the test results in Visual Studio. This can help in diagnosing what went wrong with the test.
```
### Further Exploration
Now that you've learned what property contracts are and how to write them, you might want to consider additional aspects you would like to have automated verification for your properties. These could include:

- Mutation testing: This test suite would include methods that validate that your properties behave as they should even when their input parameters are mutated slightly. This would ensure that your properties are robust.

- Boundary testing: This would mean covering the edge cases for your parameters. For example, if a property is expected to receive a number, then it could test what happens when it is given a string instead, or a really small or really large number.

- Exception testing: This would involve checking what happens if your property is given invalid input. For example, the property is expected to only handle positive numbers. If a negative number or zero is given, your property should return an error.

- Compound testing: This involves combining multiple inputs to test complex behavior. For example, setting a property to--------------------            Assert.AreEqual(Old_index, New_Ret);
	}


