A: Firstly, I believe your PUT_GetContract and PUT_SetContract tests are wrong since you're comparing the index with the returned value from ArrayList.
Secondly, you should also take into account the behavior of .Net arrays and .NET `ArrayList. In your PUT_SetContract test, you might run into an issue, because the count of ArrayList can change without affecting the ArrayList instance itself. That's why usually, the count field is not tested in the contracts.
Finally, the use of Pex to verify contracts can be slow due to the large number of random test execution. You might want to analyze the problem in more depth by executing a few test cases using Pex.
However, considering your situation the above issues might be causing some of your false-positive results.
Please note that contract testing might be considered as weak in some cases because contracts do not cover every possible situation and there are other tools such as Pex that will provide a broader coverage and may catch more bugs.

A: Contracts in .Net are a nice way to guide and verify the behavior of a function, but there is a lack of support for generic types for instance. Pex is not good at handling these scenarios and can result in false positives.
Also, make sure you are using a version of Pex that supports your .Net framework. The .Net framework can vary in terms of capabilities when interacting with contracts, so you would want to make sure that you are using a recent version of Pex that is compatible with the .Net framework you are using.
Pex might not be the best tool if it lacks feature support for your specific needs. Other strategies as well such as randomized testing, black box testing, etc can be considered as well. Also, you can consider using FxCop for performing static code analysis which can help you with your contracts and can prevent a whole class of bugs.

A: The problem was not with the Pex contracts, it was that the contract test method was not being called by the test runner due to naming convention issues (the method name didn't start with "Test"). Renaming the method to "TestPUT_GetContract" and "TestPUT_SetContract" resolved this issue.
It's also important to note that Pex can't generate random "Object" inputs, it can only handle reference types. So, in the PUT_SetContract() method where you used "Object" as the type of "value", this can lead to false positives, since Pex may randomly decide on unsupported types, which could break the method under test. It would be better to use a more specific value type. A solution could be to pass an integer as an argument instead, and perform manual conversion to object inside the method under test. 
The other considerations of the other answers also applies.

