This Pex method does not directly execute, but instead observes the states of the `ArrayList` before and after the operation and prints them to the output. It also does assertion and then the Pex observers are disabled.
You can remove it when you're ready to integrate your tests.

Now, you can run the Pex method and get coverage details for the operation you've added in ArrayList. The Get method coverage should be more than 90 percent.

## The Complete Test Code

The complete test class will look as follows:

```Csharp
[TestClass]
public partial class ArrayListContractTest
{
    // this method takes a constructor of ArrayList and index as params and performs add operation.
    [PexMethod]
    public void PexAdd(ArrayList arrList, int newElement, int index)
    {
        arrList.Add(newElement);
        if (index < arrList.Count && index >= 0)
        {
            bool testAdd = arrList.Contains(newElement);
            Assert.IsTrue(testAdd);
        }
        else
        {
            Assert.IsFalse(arrList.Contains(newElement));
        }
    }

        [PexMethod]
    public void PexAddRange(ArrayList arrList, int[] newElements)
    {
        arrList.AddRange(newElements);
        foreach (int newElement in newElements)
        {
            bool testAdd = arrList.Contains(newElement);
            Assert.IsTrue(testAdd);
        }
    }

        [PexMethod]
    public void PexInsert(ArrayList arrList, int newElement, int index)
    {
        if (index < arrList.Count && index >= 0)
        {
            arrList.Insert(index, newElement);
            bool testInsert = arrList.Contains(newElement);
            Assert.IsTrue(testInsert);
        }
        else
        {
            try { arrList.Insert(index, newElement); Assert.IsTrue(false); }
            catch (IndexOutOfRangeException) { Assert.IsTrue(true); }
        }
    }

    [PexMethod]
    [PexExplorationComplete]
    public int PUT_GetContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int index)
    {
        AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index < arrList.Count && index >= 0);
        return (int)(arrList[index]);
    }
}
    
```

Keep in mind, ArrayList is not meant to be used in testing in unit tests, you are better off using typed List<T> collection in tests due to better error handling and coverage with Pex. The sample above is provided in case you've previously implemented this functionality and want to add additional contract tests.
Also, Pex is quite robust, but not always able to catch all errors. And it might be difficult to get coverage of every possible scenario in practice. It is often a good idea, to write tests with a wide range of inputs and to understand that code well before getting everything covered by Pex.

